{
  "teamId": 158,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Milwaukee Brewers announced themselves as legitimate contenders from the very first weekend, with Ryan Braun's three-run homer on March 30th setting the tone for a team that would rely on big swings and clutch performances all year long. Fueled by the electric arrival of Christian Yelich — acquired in a winter trade that would define the franchise — and a deep, versatile lineup, Milwaukee surged through May with a blistering 19-8 record, the best month of the season, powered by timely doubles, grand slams, and late-inning heroics from an unlikely cast of contributors.\n\nThe summer brought turbulence. June and July produced losing records, and the bullpen — so often a strength — coughed up leads at critical moments, from Dan Jennings surrendering a walk-off double to Josh Bell to Joakim Soria giving up a Hunter Renfroe grand slam. Yet even through the rough patches, the Brewers never truly collapsed, winning series they needed to win and getting timely contributions from trade deadline additions like Mike Moustakas and Jonathan Schoop. The team was grinding, sometimes ugly, but never out of the race.\n\nSeptember was where Milwaukee truly caught fire, posting a stunning 19-7 record to surge past the Cubs and seize the NL Central. Jonathan Schoop's grand slam, a dominant rotation, and a bullpen anchored by Josh Hader fueled the late charge. Erik Kratz, Orlando Arcia, and other unheralded contributors delivered when the moment demanded it, embodying the scrappy, collective identity Craig Counsell had built.\n\nThe postseason brought more magic before a heartbreaking end. Milwaukee dispatched Colorado in the NLDS on the strength of Yelich and Moustakas heroics, then faced the Los Angeles Dodgers in the NLCS. Jeremy Jeffress — so dependable all year — found himself at the center of crushing moments, surrendering Justin Turner and Yasiel Puig home runs in a series that slipped away. The Brewers, 96-win darlings of the National League, fell one round short of the World Series, leaving a hungry fanbase dreaming of what was still to come.",
  "chapters": [
